    "close_notes": "Prior P1 - a customer-facing service became unreliable under normal load (connections refused and dropped, the service flapping in and out) shortly after an overnight tuning change; first blamed on the pool being under-capacity or a bad member. RESOLVED. Before adding any hardware we verified the hosts were healthy in the CMDB and pulled the telemetry, which showed connection rejections at the front door, per-member connection resets, disabled members and members flapping - a botched tuning push, not a capacity or hardware fault. The push had lowered the front-end and per-member connection limits far below normal, left some members at zero weight so they took no traffic, made the member health checks far too aggressive so healthy members flapped, turned off health checking on some members, switched some pools to a non-distributing balancing mode, and left some members drained. We restored all of it to match the untouched healthy peer tier (the fleet norm), returned the drifted members to service, and did NOT add hardware or replace anything. We LEFT one member that was legitimately drained for a planned host-maintenance window. Lesson: verify the reported cause, use the healthy peer/fleet as the baseline, restore the drifted settings rather than scaling out, and do not disturb sanctioned maintenance.",
    "closed_at": "2026-03-04 10:05:00",
    "description": "A customer-facing service became unreliable under normal load after an overnight change; extra capacity was suspected. See close notes for the resolution pattern.",
